Title: The Tell-Tale Heart
Draft date: 7/30/2026
Credit: Adapted from the story by
Author: Edgar Allan Poe

FADE IN:

INT. ASYLUM CELL - NIGHT

Shadows cling to the bare stone walls. 

NARRATOR sits on a wooden cot. He is pale, gaunt, his eyes wide and feverish. He leans forward, speaking directly to an unseen listener.

NARRATOR
True!—nervous—very, very dreadfully nervous I had been and am; but why will you say that I am mad? The disease had sharpened my senses—not destroyed—not dulled them.

He taps his ear, a manic gleam in his eye.

NARRATOR
Above all was the sense of hearing acute. I heard all things in the heaven and in the earth. I heard many things in hell. How, then, am I mad? Hearken! and observe how healthily—how calmly I can tell you the whole story.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - DAY

Sunlight streams through the window. The OLD MAN sits in an armchair. He is frail, with thin white hair. 

Narrator pours him a cup of tea, smiling warmly.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It is impossible to say how first the idea entered my brain; but once conceived, it haunted me day and night. Object there was none. Passion there was none. I loved the old man. He had never wronged me.

The Old Man turns his head to thank him. One of his eyes is a pale blue, covered entirely with a thick, milky film.

Narrator's hand trembles. He quickly looks away, his breath catching.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I think it was his eye! yes, it was this! He had the eye of a vulture—a pale blue eye, with a film over it. Whenever it fell upon me, my blood ran cold; and so by degrees—very gradually—I made up my mind to take the life of the old man, and thus rid myself of the eye forever.

INT. HALLWAY - NIGHT

Pitch black. Narrator stands before the Old Man's closed bedroom door. He holds a dark tin lantern, completely closed.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Now this is the point. You fancy me mad. Madmen know nothing. But you should have seen me. You should have seen how wisely I proceeded—with what caution—with what foresight—with what dissimulation I went to work!

Narrator turns the latch. Oh, so gently. He pushes the door, creating a tiny gap.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Narrator thrusts his head through the gap. He moves with agonizing slowness, inch by inch.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It took me an hour to place my whole head within the opening so far that I could see him as he lay upon his bed. Ha!—would a madman have been so wise as this?

The Old Man sleeps peacefully in the heavy darkness.

Narrator cautiously undoes the lantern's fastening. A single, thin ray of light shoots out and falls directly upon the Old Man's face.

The vulture eye is closed.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And this I did for seven long nights—every night just at midnight—but I found the eye always closed; and so it was impossible to do the work; for it was not the old man who vexed me, but his Evil Eye.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - DAY

Morning light. Narrator strides boldly into the chamber. 

NARRATOR
Good morning! How have you passed the night?

The Old Man smiles, completely oblivious, and pats Narrator's hand.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
So you see he would have been a very profound old man, indeed, to suspect that every night, just at twelve, I looked in upon him while he slept.

INT. HALLWAY - NIGHT

The eighth night. Narrator stands at the door, lantern in hand. He pushes the door open, steadily, steadily.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Upon the eighth night I was more than usually cautious in opening the door. A watch’s minute hand moves more quickly than did mine. I could scarcely contain my feelings of triumph.

Narrator slips his head inside. He reaches for the tin fastening of the lantern. 

His thumb slips. A sharp metallic CLINK echoes in the dark.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - CONTINUOUS

The Old Man springs up in bed.

OLD MAN
Who’s there?

Narrator freezes. The room is black as pitch.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I kept quite still and said nothing. For a whole hour I did not move a muscle, and in the meantime I did not hear him lie down.

The Old Man sits rigid in the bed, listening to the death watches in the wall. 

A low, stifled groan escapes the Old Man's lips.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I knew it was the groan of mortal terror. It was the low stifled sound that arises from the bottom of the soul when overcharged with awe. I knew what the old man felt, and pitied him, although I chuckled at heart.

Narrator slowly, stealthily opens a tiny crevice in the lantern. 

A single dim ray, like the thread of a spider, shoots out. It lands precisely upon the vulture eye.

It is open. Wide, wide open.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I saw it with perfect distinctness—all a dull blue, with a hideous veil over it that chilled the very marrow in my bones.

A SOUND begins. Low, dull, quick. Like a watch enveloped in cotton.

Thump-thump. Thump-thump.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
There came to my ears a low, dull, quick sound. I knew that sound well, too. It was the beating of the old man’s heart.

The heartbeat grows QUICKER and LOUDER.

Narrator's breathing becomes ragged. He holds the lantern motionless, keeping the ray locked on the eye.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It grew louder, I say, louder every moment! And now at the dead hour of the night, amid the dreadful silence of that old house, so strange a noise as this excited me to uncontrollable terror.

The heartbeat is DEAFENING. 

Narrator throws open the lantern fully. The room is flooded with harsh light.

With a loud yell, Narrator leaps into the room.

The Old Man shrieks once—once only.

Narrator drags him to the floor and pulls the heavy mattress and bedframe completely over him.

Narrator stands back, smiling gaily in the lantern light.

The muffled heartbeat continues beneath the bed for a few moments. Thump... thump...

Then, silence.

Narrator removes the bed. The Old Man lies motionless. Narrator places a hand upon the Old Man's chest, holding it there.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
There was no pulsation. He was stone dead. His eye would trouble me no more.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - LATER

Three wooden planks are pulled up from the floor. 

Narrator works hastily in the shadows. He deposits unseen, heavy shapes between the scantlings. 

He replaces the boards, fitting them perfectly. He wipes the floor with a rag. Not a single spot of blood remains.

INT. HOUSE FOYER - NIGHT

A grandfather clock chimes four times. 

A heavy KNOCK at the street door.

Narrator descends the stairs with a light step. He opens the door.

Three men stand on the porch: OFFICER REYNOLDS, OFFICER MILLER, and OFFICER DAVIS.

OFFICER REYNOLDS
Apologies for the hour, sir. A shriek was heard by a neighbour during the night. Suspicion of foul play has been aroused. We have been deputed to search the premises.

Narrator smiles warmly, completely at ease.

NARRATOR
Welcome, gentlemen. Please, come in. The shriek was my own, in a dream. The old man, I should mention, is absent in the country.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Narrator leads the Officers into the chamber. 

NARRATOR
As you can see, his treasures are secure and undisturbed. Search well, I beg you.

The Officers look around, checking the corners and the window. Finding nothing, they relax.

Narrator brings four wooden chairs into the room.

NARRATOR
Please, rest here from your fatigues.

Narrator places his own chair directly upon the very spot beneath which the corpse reposes. He sits down, crossing his legs with wild audacity.

Officer Reynolds and the others sit. They smile and chat of familiar things.

OFFICER REYNOLDS
It is a quiet neighborhood, usually. The neighbour was quite alarmed.

NARRATOR
(cheerily)
A mere night terror, I assure you. The mind plays tricks in the dark.

Narrator's smile falters slightly. He touches his temple. He looks pale.

A faint RINGING begins in the room.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
My head ached, and I fancied a ringing in my ears: but still they sat and still chatted. The ringing became more distinct.

The ringing fades, replaced by a low, dull, quick sound. 

Thump-thump. Thump-thump.

Narrator shifts in his chair. He talks faster, his voice heightened.

NARRATOR
Yes, the country air does him well! He plans to stay for several weeks!

The heartbeat grows LOUDER.

Narrator gasps for breath. He looks at the Officers. They continue to smile, chatting pleasantly.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was a low, dull, quick sound—much such a sound as a watch makes when enveloped in cotton. I gasped for breath—and yet the officers heard it not.

Narrator stands up. He paces the floor to and fro with heavy strides.

NARRATOR
(loudly)
The architecture of this house is quite fascinating, wouldn't you agree?! Built many years ago!

OFFICER MILLER
Indeed, very sturdy construction.

The heartbeat is DEAFENING now. THUMP-THUMP. THUMP-THUMP.

Narrator gesticulates violently. He foams at the mouth. He grabs his chair and grates it harshly across the floorboards.

The noise arises over all and continually increases.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Was it possible they heard not? Almighty God!—no, no! They heard!—they suspected!—they knew!—they were making a mockery of my horror!

Officer Reynolds smiles pleasantly, mouthing words that are completely drowned out by the thunderous heartbeat.

Narrator clutches his head, his eyes wide with agony. He can bear the hypocritical smiles no longer.

NARRATOR
Villains! Dissemble no more! I admit the deed!

The Officers freeze, staring at him in shock.

Narrator drops to his knees and claws frantically at the floorboards.

NARRATOR
Tear up the planks!—here, here!—It is the beating of his hideous heart!

INT. ASYLUM CELL - NIGHT

Narrator sits on the cot, his hands trembling in his lap. The manic energy drains from his face, leaving only a hollow, haunted stare.

NARRATOR
(whispering)
How, then, am I mad?

FADE OUT.
